      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · the manifold-atlas matheme (Derived in its home; the crossing Offered)",
          "title": "A crossing has a rule — the circle's two charts",
          "italic": "The example demonstrates local pictures disagreeing in written values while agreeing in what those values describe — the overlap and transformation explain the agreement.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The mathematical witness is Offered for the reconstructive proposal; the constructions retain the Derived standing of their matheme homes. Two charts cover the circle by omitting opposite poles. At the point (x,y)=(3/5,4/5), their coordinates are u=3 and v=1/3; on the overlap the rule is v=1/u. The reports differ numerically because they use different coordinates; the declared transformation identifies their common point. Height is 4/5 in either account. Even motion must be",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "transformed: when du/dt=2 at this point, dv/dt=−2/9, and both descriptions give the same change of height — giving both coordinate velocities the number two would change the motion described. Conversely, when the inverse is missing, the common domain absent, or regularity fails, the conclusion does not follow. The compact circle, sphere and torus cannot each be covered by one global chart into an open Euclidean domain of the same dimension; compactness gives the obstruction.\n\nOccurrences: the two charts and omitted poles (#2); the shared point and v=1/u (#2); the transformed velocity (#2); the compactness obstruction (#2, contrasted with the disk at #2b).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
